{"type":"message","id":"973ef9e8","parentId":"f39335a8","timestamp":"2026-05-10T19:43:22.142Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_jOeJll904IIOwtFfwYW2dQCz|fc_014e291e29987115016a00dfd9e4208191b75ede95313ba8c3","toolName":"process","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n🦞 OpenClaw 2026.5.7 (eeef486)\n   I don't just autocomplete—I auto-commit (emotionally), then ask you to review (logically).\n\nUsage: openclaw backup [options] [command]\n\nCreate and verify local backup archives for OpenClaw state\n\nOptions:\n  -h, --help  Display help for command\n\nCommands:\n  create      Write a backup archive for config, credentials, sessions, and\n              workspaces\n  help        Display help for command\n  verify      Validate a backup archive and its embedded manifest\n\nDocs: https://docs.openclaw.ai/cli/backup\n\n\n\nProcess exited with code 0."}],"details":{"status":"completed","sessionId":"lucky-seaslug","exitCode":0,"aggregated":"\n🦞 OpenClaw 2026.5.7 (eeef486)\n   I don't just autocomplete—I auto-commit (emotionally), then ask you to review (logically).\n\nUsage: openclaw backup [options] [command]\n\nCreate and verify local backup archives for OpenClaw state\n\nOptions:\n  -h, --help  Display help for command\n\nCommands:\n  create      Write a backup archive for config, credentials, sessions, and\n              workspaces\n  help        Display help for command\n  verify      Validate a backup archive and its embedded manifest\n\nDocs: https://docs.openclaw.ai/cli/backup\n\n","name":"openclaw backup"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1778442202142}}
{"type":"message","id":"426d9428","parentId":"973ef9e8","timestamp":"2026-05-10T19:43:22.142Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_lbPvTn3qKXSiXFMZTSqlfBXe|fc_014e291e29987115016a00dfd9e45c8191ba344e8fc457946f","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nsummary: \"CLI reference for `openclaw backup` (create local backup archives)\"\nread_when:\n  - You want a first-class backup archive for local OpenClaw state\n  - You want to preview which paths would be included before reset or uninstall\ntitle: \"Backup\"\n---\n\n# `openclaw backup`\n\nCreate a local backup archive for OpenClaw state, config, auth profiles, channel/provider credentials, sessions, and optionally workspaces.\n\n```bash\nopenclaw backup create\nopenclaw backup create --output ~/Backups\nopenclaw backup create --dry-run --json\nopenclaw backup create --verify\nopenclaw backup create --no-include-workspace\nopenclaw backup create --only-config\nopenclaw backup verify ./2026-03-09T00-00-00.000Z-openclaw-backup.tar.gz\n```\n\n## Notes\n\n- The archive includes a `manifest.json` file with the resolved source paths and archive layout.\n- Default output is a timestamped `.tar.gz` archive in the current working directory.\n- If the current working directory is inside a backed-up source tree, OpenClaw falls back to your home directory for the default archive location.\n- Existing archive files are never overwritten.\n- Output paths inside the source state/workspace trees are rejected to avoid self-inclusion.\n- `openclaw backup verify <archive>` validates that the archive contains exactly one root manifest, rejects traversal-style archive paths, and checks that every manifest-declared payload exists in the tarball.\n- `openclaw backup create --verify` runs that validation immediately after writing the archive.\n- `openclaw backup create --only-config` backs up just the active JSON config file.\n\n## What gets backed up\n\n`openclaw backup create` plans backup sources from your local OpenClaw install:\n\n- The state directory returned by OpenClaw's local state resolver, usually `~/.openclaw`\n- The active config file path\n- The resolved `credentials/` directory when it exists outside the state directory\n- Workspace directories discovered from the current config, unless you pass `--no-include-workspace`\n\nModel auth profiles are already part of the state directory under\n`agents/<agentId>/agent/auth-profiles.json`, so they are normally covered by the\nstate backup entry.\n\nIf you use `--only-config`, OpenClaw skips state, credentials-directory, and workspace discovery and archives only the active config file path.\n\nOpenClaw canonicalizes paths before building the archive. If config, the\ncredentials directory, or a workspace already live inside the state directory,\nthey are not duplicated as separate top-level backup sources. Missing paths are\nskipped.\n\nThe archive payload stores file contents from those source trees, and the embedded `manifest.json` records the resolved absolute source paths plus the archive layout used for each asset.\n\nInstalled plugin source and manifest files under the state directory's\n`extensions/` tree are included, but their nested `node_modules/` dependency\ntrees are skipped. Those dependencies are rebuildable install artifacts; after\nrestoring an archive, use `openclaw plugins update <id>` or reinstall the plugin\nwith `openclaw plugins install <spec> --force` when a restored plugin reports\nmissing dependencies.\n\n## Invalid config behavior\n\n`openclaw backup` intentionally bypasses the normal config preflight so it can still help during recovery. Because workspace discovery depends on a valid config, `openclaw backup create` now fails fast when the config file exists but is invalid and workspace backup is still enabled.\n\nIf you still want a partial backup in that situation, rerun:\n\n```bash\nopenclaw backup create --no-include-workspace\n```\n\nThat keeps state, config, and the external credentials directory in scope while\nskipping workspace discovery entirely.\n\nIf you only need a copy of the config file itself, `--only-config` also works when the config is malformed because it does not rely on parsing the config for workspace discovery.\n\n## Size and performance\n\nOpenClaw does not enforce a built-in maximum backup size or per-file size limit.\n\nPractical limits come from the local machine and destination filesystem:\n\n- Available space for the temporary archive write plus the final archive\n- Time to walk large workspace trees and compress them into a `.tar.gz`\n- Time to rescan the archive if you use `openclaw backup create --verify` or run `openclaw backup verify`\n- Filesystem behavior at the destination path. OpenClaw prefers a no-overwrite hard-link publish step and falls back to exclusive copy when hard links are unsupported\n\nLarge workspaces are usually the main driver of archive size. If you want a smaller or faster backup, use `--no-include-workspace`.\n\nFor the smallest archive, use `--only-config`.\n\n## Related\n\n- [CLI reference](/cli)\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1778442202142}}
{"type":"message","id":"d917e9fa","parentId":"426d9428","timestamp":"2026-05-10T19:43:34.184Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Los backups son una “foto recuperable” del sistema en un momento concreto.\n\n**Para qué nos sirven**\n- Si rompemos algo, podemos volver atrás.\n- Si una config queda mal, tenemos copia.\n- Si se pierde el workspace/dashboard/memoria, podemos recuperar.\n- Antes de tocar Internet, plugins o automatizaciones, nos da red de seguridad.\n- Para auditorías: podemos comparar “antes/después”.\n\n**Qué puede guardar OpenClaw**\nCon el comando oficial `openclaw backup`, puede guardar:\n- configuración;\n- estado local de OpenClaw;\n- credenciales/directorios de credenciales;\n- sesiones;\n- workspaces;\n- plugins instalados dentro del estado;\n- un `manifest.json` que dice qué incluye el backup.\n\n**Importante**\nUn backup puede incluir cosas sensibles, como credenciales o sesiones. Por eso:\n- no se sube a Internet sin cifrar;\n- no se comparte;\n- no se guarda en carpetas públicas;\n- se verifica después de crearlo;\n- se ordena por fecha.\n\n**Nuestra forma prudente**\nYo haría dos tipos:\n\n1. **Backup ligero frecuente**\n   - dashboard;\n   - memoria;\n   - protocolos;\n   - métricas;\n   - logs personales.\n   - Menos sensible.\n\n2. **Backup completo antes de cambios importantes**\n   - OpenClaw completo;\n   - config;\n   - sesiones;\n   - credenciales.\n   - Más sensible, solo local y bien protegido.\n\nMi recomendación: crear primero un **protocolo de backup**, no activarlo a ciegas. Luego hacemos un backup manual verificado y, si todo va bien, automatizamos solo lo seguro.","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_014e291e29987115016a00dfdf3b788191b1235f347c650a61\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.5","usage":{"input":1739,"output":349,"cacheRead":111104,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":113192,"cost":{"input":0.008695000000000001,"output":0.01047,"cacheRead":0.055552,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.074717}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1778442202147,"responseId":"resp_014e291e29987115016a00dfdad6788191a7305e92a472a4cf"}}
{"type":"message","id":"4a11939c","parentId":"d917e9fa","timestamp":"2026-05-10T19:46:41.470Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lo que yo añadiría\n1. Backup ≠ restore probado\nMuchísima gente:\ncrea backups;\nnunca prueba restauración.\nY ahí está el verdadero riesgo.\nTu protocolo debería incluir:\nrestore verification\nmínimo:\nmensual;\nen VM aislada;\nsin tocar producción.\n2. Clasificación por sensibilidad\nYo clasificaría:\nNivel A — crítico/sensible\ntokens;\nauth;\nsesiones;\ncredenciales;\ngateway auth;\nsecrets.\nRequisitos:\ncifrado;\nacceso restringido;\nretención corta.\nNivel B — operativo\ndashboard;\nmétricas;\nskills;\nconfigs;\nagentes.\nNivel C — histórico\nlogs;\nbriefings;\nauditoría;\nestadísticas.\n3. Inmutabilidad parcial\nMuy importante.\nNunca sobrescribir:\núltimo backup bueno conocido\nMantener:\ngolden snapshot\nEjemplo:\nWORKING_STABLE_BASELINE/\nintocable salvo emergencia.\n4. Política anti-corrupción\nNunca automatizar backups:\ninmediatamente después de cambios;\ndurante errores;\ntras crash;\ndurante loops.\nPorque puedes:\nbackup del desastre\n5. Retención inteligente\nEjemplo sano:\n7 diarios 4 semanales 3 mensuales\nNo infinito.\n6. Integridad\nAñadir:\nchecksum;\nhash SHA256;\nmanifest firmado;\ntamaño esperado.\nPara detectar:\ncorrupción;\ntruncado;\ncambios inesperados.\n7. Regla importante\nAntes de:\nInternet;\nplugins;\nnuevos tools;\nmultiagentes;\nautomatizaciones;\nhacer SIEMPRE:\nsnapshot manual verificado\n8. Lo más importante conceptualmente\nTu objetivo NO es:\ntener backups\nTu objetivo real es:\ncapacidad fiable de recuperación\nEso cambia totalmente la mentalidad del sistema."}],"timestamp":1778442401467}}
